===================Tim Mooney
[mooney@dogbert.cc.ndsu.nodak.edu]=================
I've heard of MRU too, but I've never used it.  I can tell you that the
CLCM subset includes the `mcutil' command, which is all you need to test
basic functionality of your jukebox.  That command is handy to know even
after you get NetWorker installed and working, since `nsrjb' doesn't know
how to show you the contents of the load ports, while mcutil does.

We use the following /etc/mcicap entry with our STK9710:

obelisk|STK9710|mc0:\
    :mc=/dev/mc30:\
    :dn=rmt0h,rmt1h,rmt2h:tc=tl810:

You'll of course need to change just about everything there.  ;-)  About
the only thing that you will keep is the `tc=tl810', which is all mcutil
needs to figure out how to get the actual information about
slots/ports/drives
from the jukebox.

Maybe the MRU provides something really neat that I've been missing, but as
far as I can tell the CLCM subset gives you everything you *need*.
============================================================================
====



